{
  "spec": "paladin-holy",
  "state": "written",
  "notes": "Written for the fork's healing sim (sim/healsim: the healer is raid player 0, players 1-4 its party, player 5 the tank). Flash of Light is the staple, Holy Light covers a tank below 40 percent behind Divine Favor, and the instant Holy Shock and a second Holy Light line spend mana only while it is no further behind the fight clock than five points (currentManaPercent against remainingTimePercent). Measured on the fork's healsim under the site heal profile (data/curated/heal-profile.json) with healing gear of roughly 500 healing power, 250 intellect and 20 mp5: a healer short of mana, the usual case, never reaches the paced lines and heals as a Flash of Light healer, the best variant tried (Holy Shock at a fixed threshold cost about 8 percent of the effective healing, Holy Light below 70 percent about 1 percent); with a large mana pool the paced lines add about 13 percent effective HPS at under 1 percent overheal. Light's Vigil (a party heal through the next Holy Shock on its target, 1340 mana at level 60) is in the engine but left out of this rotation: it lowered effective HPS by 2 to 9 percent in every variant tried, because the profile's raid pulses (450 damage on three members) are too small for a five-target heal that costs as much mana as almost ten Flash of Lights. Downranking is not used: the client states no level penalty on a lower rank's healing power, so the sim would flatter rank 4 to 6 Holy Light beyond what the game allows. Prepull Greater Blessing of Light (raid-wide): it adds bonus healing to Holy Light and Flash of Light that counts through each spell's coefficient, an assumption, since the client states only the amount. Spirit regeneration, mp5 and the five second rule are the engine's own formulas (the client publishes no spirit regeneration table). Every healer number comes from the engine's fake raid, not from a boss fight.",
